WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.140
Exceptions, RTTI, and dynamic casting can depend on compatible compiler, runtime, and ABI assumptions.

2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.760
They may be appropriate inside one component, but they should not casually cross every boundary.

3
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:24.140
A C API, foreign language interface, plugin boundary, thread entry point, or ABI-sensitive library boundary may need a different failure reporting rule.

4
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:31.040
If an exception escapes into code that cannot handle it, the result may be termination or lost diagnostic context.

5
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:39.080
A safer boundary pattern is to catch implementation exceptions at the boundary and translate them into a stable status code or error object.

6
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.740
The boundary should preserve useful diagnostic context safely, without exposing internal details that callers should not rely on.

7
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:53.520
It should also define object state after failure so callers know whether cleanup, retry, or shutdown is appropriate.

8
00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:00.040
RTTI and dynamic casting should be used only when the boundary contract supports compatible runtime assumptions.

9
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:10.060
If modules can be built independently, versioned capability checks or explicit type identifiers may be safer than relying on cross-module C++ runtime behavior.

10
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:20.720
The key design principle is simple: keep implementation error mechanisms inside the component unless the ABI contract explicitly says they are supported across the boundary.

11
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.500
That contract should also say how diagnostics are collected without leaking private implementation details.

12
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.820
Error translation also needs lifecycle rules.

13
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:39.760
Decide who owns an error object, how long a diagnostic string remains valid, whether the error state is thread-local, and how callers clear or copy it.

14
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:47.940
These details may sound small, but they prevent failures at the moment when the system is already on an unusual path and needs predictable recovery behavior.
